    q: 'Is an MBA a good option after a science degree?',
    a: 'Yes, and science graduates are eligible for every major MBA entrance exam (CAT, XAT, CMAT) with no restriction on undergraduate subject. The outcome depends almost entirely on the institute: a strong institute can move a science graduate into consulting, product, or analytics roles that a plain MSc rarely reaches on its own, while a weak institute on a loan is a real financial risk. See the full trade-off in the linked MSc vs MBA comparison before deciding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does an actuary earn in India, and how long does it take to qualify?',
    a: 'A partly-qualified fresher actuary who has cleared 2-3 IAI exams typically earns Rs 6-10 LPA. Pay rises with each set of exams cleared, reaching Rs 12-25 LPA at Associate level and Rs 30-60 LPA once fully Fellow-qualified. Most people take 5-10 years to clear the full 13-15 exam sequence while working, so this route pays well but demands sustained, multi-year exam discipline, not a fast payoff.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is data science a realistic postgraduate route for a non-Computer-Science science graduate?',
    a: 'Yes, especially with a Maths, Statistics, or Physics background, since these subjects already build the quantitative reasoning the field needs. MSc or PG Diploma programs in Data Science are offered through entrance routes like GATE, CUET PG, and IIT JAM at institutions including IIT Madras, IIT Delhi, and the Indian Statistical Institute, with fresher salaries commonly in the Rs 6-15 LPA range, higher at the strongest institutes.',
  },
