          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Always confirm current eligibility percentages, exam dates, and stipend figures on the official CSIR
    HRDG, GATE, JEST, and UPSC portals before you start preparing, since cut-offs and amounts are revised
    periodically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mba">The MBA pivot for science graduates</h2>

  <p>
    An MBA is not a science-specific route, but enough science graduates consider it that it deserves an
    honest place on this list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Science graduates are eligible for every major MBA entrance exam (CAT, XAT, CMAT) with no restriction
    on undergraduate subject, and a genuinely strong institute can move a science background into
    consulting, product management, or business analytics roles that a plain MSc rarely reaches directly.
    The catch is the same one that applies to every other subject: the institute decides almost everything,
    and a weak or unranked MBA on a loan is a real financial risk next to a well-chosen MSc plus one
    certification.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>A useful planning check, not a universal rule:</strong> treat roughly 10% of your family's
      total realistic education budget as the strict default ceiling for a single postgraduate course,
      including an MBA. Spending materially more on a top institute is only justified by course-specific
      evidence, a verified placement report for science-background students at that institute, faculty
      and specialisation depth in your target function, and a loan EMI that clearly clears against
      realistic starting pay, not by the institute's ranking or brand alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      If you have already narrowed the decision down to this exact fork, MSc versus MBA, read the full
      comparison in <a href="/blog/college-degrees/msc-vs-mba-india/">MSc vs MBA India</a> for the deeper
      cost, timeline, and outcome breakdown by subject.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="certifications">Certifications worth the money</h2>

  <p>
    Not every strong postgraduate move is a two-year degree. Three certification-led routes show up
    repeatedly across the subjects above, and each earns its place for a different reason.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Actuarial science</p>
    <p>
      Entry runs through the ACET exam, followed by 13-15 IAI exams across Core Technical, Core
      Principles, Specialist, and Specialist Advanced stages. It is exam-heavy, not classroom-heavy, and
      India currently has fewer than 500 qualified Fellows against a fast-growing demand in health,
      reinsurance, and analytics, which is why the pay curve stays steep at every qualification stage.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Data science</p>
    <p>
      The stronger versions of this route run through university-affiliated MSc or PG Diploma programs
      with a real entrance exam (GATE, CUET PG, IIT JAM), not a loose online-only certificate. A Maths,
      Statistics, or Physics base makes the coursework considerably easier and the eventual salary range
      (commonly Rs 6-15 LPA at entry) considerably more reliable.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Biotech regulatory affairs and clinical research</p>
    <p>
      Most programs here run 6-12 months with no entrance exam, built for Life Sciences, Biotechnology, or
      Pharmacy graduates specifically. The realistic pay curve moves slowly at entry (Rs 2.4-4.5 LPA) but
      climbs meaningfully once you are inside a Clinical Research Associate or regulatory role, particularly
      with added training in FDA, EMA, or ICH-style global standards.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    Eight routes across five subjects is still too many to compare in your head at once. The
    4-Checkpoint Protocol narrows it down fast.
  </p>

